<!-- l4: decide:the purpose falls within paragraph (1) -->

**26.** In relation to a Purpose “purpose”, the purpose falls within paragraph (1) if—

    (a) (1) For the purposes of this Law, the charitable purposes are –;

    (b) (a) — the prevention or relief of poverty with purpose;

    (c) (b) — the advancement of education with purpose;

    (d) (c) — the advancement of religion with purpose;

    (e) (d) — the advancement of health with purpose;

    (f) (e) — the saving of lives with purpose;

    (g) (f) — the advancement of citizenship or community development with purpose;

    (h) (g) — the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science with purpose;

    (i) (h) — the advancement of public participation in sport with purpose;

    (j) (i) — the provision of recreational facilities or activities with purpose;

    (k) (j) — the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation with purpose;

    (l) (k) — the promotion of religious or racial harmony with purpose;

    (m) (l) — the promotion of equality and diversity with purpose;

    (n) (m) — the advancement of environmental protection or improvement with purpose;

    (o) (n) — the relief of those in need with purpose;

    (p) (o) — the advancement of animal welfare with purpose; or

    (q) (p) — any other analogous purpose with purpose.

<!-- l4: decide:(5) — the purpose is excluded as political -->

**27.** In relation to a Purpose “purpose”, (5) — the purpose is excluded as political if—

    (a) (5); and

    (b) purpose's for advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere.

<!-- l4: decide:the purpose is a charitable purpose -->

**28.** In relation to a Purpose “purpose”, the purpose is a charitable purpose if—

    (a) the purpose falls within paragraph (1) with purpose; and

    (b) (5) — the purpose is not excluded as political with purpose.

## Article 7 — Public benefit

<!-- l4: decide:7(3)(b) — the entity benefits only identified natural persons -->

**29.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 7(3)(b) — the entity benefits only identified natural persons if—

    (a) (3)(b); and

    (b) entity's the public benefit finding's benefit provided only to one particular natural person or to a group of identified natural persons.

<!-- l4: decide:7(2) — the mandatory considerations were had regard to -->

**30.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 7(2) — the mandatory considerations were had regard to if—

    (a) (2) The person determining the question must have regard to –;

    (b) (a) how any – (i) benefit gained or likely to be gained by members of the entity or any other persons (other than as members of the public), and (ii) disbenefit incurred or likely to be incurred by the public, in consequence of the entity exercising its functions, compares with the benefit gained or likely to be gained by the public in that consequence; and;

    (c) entity's the public benefit finding's regard had to the comparison required by Article 7(2)(a);

    (d) (b) if benefit is, or is likely to be, provided to a section of the public only, whether any condition on obtaining that benefit (including any charge or fee) is unduly restrictive.; and

    (e) 7(2)(b) — the section-of-the-public question was reached with entity.

<!-- l4: decide:7(2)(b) — the section-of-the-public question was reached -->

**31.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 7(2)(b) — the section-of-the-public question was reached if—

    (a) it is not the case that entity's the public benefit finding's benefit provided to a section of the public only; or

    (b) entity's the public benefit finding's regard had to the question required by Article 7(2)(b).

<!-- l4: decide:7(3)(a) — no charitable purpose was presumed to be for the public benefit -->

**32.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 7(3)(a) — no charitable purpose was presumed to be for the public benefit if—

    (a) (3)(a); and

    (b) it is not the case that entity's the public benefit finding's a particular charitable purpose presumed to be for the public benefit.

<!-- l4: decide:the public benefit determination was properly made -->

**33.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, the public benefit determination was properly made if—

    (a) 7(2) — the mandatory considerations were had regard to with entity; and

    (b) 7(3)(a) — no charitable purpose was presumed to be for the public benefit with entity.

## Article 5 — The charity test

<!-- l4: decide:the entity has at least one charitable purpose -->

**34.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, the entity has at least one charitable purpose means any with the purpose is a charitable purpose and entity's purposes.

<!-- l4: decide:(ii) — the purpose is purely ancillary or incidental -->

**35.** In relation to an Entity “entity” and a Purpose “purpose”, (ii) — the purpose is purely ancillary or incidental if—

    (a) (ii);

    (b) purpose's purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes;

    (c) the entity has at least one charitable purpose with entity; and

    (d) (5) — the purpose is not excluded as political with purpose.

<!-- l4: decide:the purpose is charitable, or purely ancillary or incidental -->

**36.** In relation to an Entity “entity” and a Purpose “purpose”, the purpose is charitable, or purely ancillary or incidental if—

    (a) (i);

    (b) the purpose is a charitable purpose with purpose; or

    (c) (ii) — the purpose is purely ancillary or incidental with entity and purpose.

<!-- l4: decide:5(1)(a) — all of its purposes are charitable or ancillary -->

**37.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 5(1)(a) — all of its purposes are charitable or ancillary if—

    (a) (a); and

    (b) all with given purpose then the purpose is charitable, or purely ancillary or incidental with entity and purpose and entity's purposes.

<!-- l4: decide:the entity provides or intends to provide public benefit -->

**38.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, the entity provides or intends to provide public benefit if—

    (a) entity's the public benefit finding's public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere, to a reasonable degree, provided in giving effect to those purposes; or

    (b) the applicant's intention limb with entity.

<!-- l4: decide:the applicant's intention limb -->

**39.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, the applicant's intention limb if—

    (a) entity's an applicant; and

    (b) entity's the public benefit finding's public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere, to a reasonable degree, intended to be provided in giving effect to those purposes.

<!-- l4: decide:5(1)(b) — public benefit -->

**40.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 5(1)(b) — public benefit if—

    (a) (b);

    (b) the entity provides or intends to provide public benefit with entity; and

    (c) it is not the case that 7(3)(b) — the entity benefits only identified natural persons with entity.

<!-- l4: decide:5(2) — direction or control by a listed person is expressly permitted -->

**41.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 5(2) — direction or control by a listed person is expressly permitted if—

    (a) all of the following apply—

        (i) (a); and

        (ii) entity's constitution's express permission for the entity's activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by a Minister;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b); and

        (ii) entity's constitution's express permission for the entity's activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by a member of the States Assembly; or

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c); and

        (ii) entity's constitution's express permission for the entity's activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by any equivalent of such a person in another jurisdiction.

<!-- l4: decide:5(2) — governorship by a listed person is expressly permitted -->

**42.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 5(2) — governorship by a listed person is expressly permitted if—

    (a) all of the following apply—

        (i) (a); and

        (ii) entity's constitution's express permission for any of the entity's governors to be a Minister;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b); and

        (ii) entity's constitution's express permission for any of the entity's governors to be a member of the States Assembly; or

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c); and

        (ii) entity's constitution's express permission for any of the entity's governors to be any equivalent of such a person in another jurisdiction.

<!-- l4: decide:5(2) — a listed person is expressly permitted under either mode -->

**43.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 5(2) — a listed person is expressly permitted under either mode if—

    (a) 5(2) — direction or control by a listed person is expressly permitted with entity; or

    (b) 5(2) — governorship by a listed person is expressly permitted with entity.

<!-- l4: decide:5(2) — the government-control disqualification applies -->

**44.** In relation to an Entity “entity”, 5(2) — the government-control disqualification applies if—

    (a) (2);

    (b) 5(2) — a listed person is expressly permitted under either mode with entity;

    (c) entity's constitution's that permission extended to such a person acting in that capacity;

    (d) (3); and

    (e) it is not the case that entity's specified in an Order under Article 5(3) disapplying paragraph (2).

<!-- l4: decide:the entity meets the charity test -->

**45. export Decide whether an entity meets the charity test in Article 5 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, applying the charitable-purposes heads in Article 6 (including the political-purposes carve-out), the public-benefit rules in Article 7, and the government-control disqualification in Article 5(2).**

In relation to an Entity “entity”, the entity meets the charity test if—

    (a) 5(1)(a) — all of its purposes are charitable or ancillary with entity;

    (b) 5(1)(b) — public benefit with entity; and

    (c) it is not the case that 5(2) — the government-control disqualification applies with entity.

## Scenarios

<!-- l4: decide:a poverty-relief purpose -->

**46.** A poverty-relief purpose means Purpose where description is the prevention and relief of poverty in the Parish of St Helier, the prevention or relief of poverty is TRUE, the advancement of education is FALSE, the advancement of religion is FALSE, the advancement of health is FALSE, the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering is FALSE, the saving of lives is FALSE, the advancement of citizenship or community development is FALSE, rural or urban regeneration is FALSE, the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities is FALSE, the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science is FALSE, the advancement of public participation in sport is FALSE, sport that involves physical skill and exertion is FALSE, the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom they are primarily intended is FALSE, primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large is FALSE, the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation is FALSE, the promotion of religious or racial harmony is FALSE, the promotion of equality and diversity is FALSE, the advancement of environmental protection or improvement is FALSE, the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, the advancement of animal welfare is FALSE, reasonably regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o) is FALSE, the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god) is FALSE, for advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere is FALSE and purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes is FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:a sickness-prevention purpose -->

**47.** A sickness-prevention purpose means Purpose where description is the prevention of communicable disease, the prevention or relief of poverty is FALSE, the advancement of education is FALSE, the advancement of religion is FALSE, the advancement of health is FALSE, the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering is TRUE, the saving of lives is FALSE, the advancement of citizenship or community development is FALSE, rural or urban regeneration is FALSE, the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities is FALSE, the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science is FALSE, the advancement of public participation in sport is FALSE, sport that involves physical skill and exertion is FALSE, the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom they are primarily intended is FALSE, primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large is FALSE, the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation is FALSE, the promotion of religious or racial harmony is FALSE, the promotion of equality and diversity is FALSE, the advancement of environmental protection or improvement is FALSE, the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, the advancement of animal welfare is FALSE, reasonably regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o) is FALSE, the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god) is FALSE, for advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere is FALSE and purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes is FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:a spectator-pastime purpose -->

**48.** A spectator-pastime purpose means Purpose where description is the advancement of public participation in competitive pigeon fancying, the prevention or relief of poverty is FALSE, the advancement of education is FALSE, the advancement of religion is FALSE, the advancement of health is FALSE, the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering is FALSE, the saving of lives is FALSE, the advancement of citizenship or community development is FALSE, rural or urban regeneration is FALSE, the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities is FALSE, the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science is FALSE, the advancement of public participation in sport is TRUE, sport that involves physical skill and exertion is FALSE, the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom they are primarily intended is FALSE, primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large is FALSE, the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation is FALSE, the promotion of religious or racial harmony is FALSE, the promotion of equality and diversity is FALSE, the advancement of environmental protection or improvement is FALSE, the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, the advancement of animal welfare is FALSE, reasonably regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o) is FALSE, the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god) is FALSE, for advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere is FALSE and purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes is FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:a physical-sport purpose -->

**49.** A physical-sport purpose means Purpose where description is the advancement of public participation in island swimming, the prevention or relief of poverty is FALSE, the advancement of education is FALSE, the advancement of religion is FALSE, the advancement of health is FALSE, the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering is FALSE, the saving of lives is FALSE, the advancement of citizenship or community development is FALSE, rural or urban regeneration is FALSE, the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities is FALSE, the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science is FALSE, the advancement of public participation in sport is TRUE, sport that involves physical skill and exertion is TRUE, the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom they are primarily intended is FALSE, primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large is FALSE, the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation is FALSE, the promotion of religious or racial harmony is FALSE, the promotion of equality and diversity is FALSE, the advancement of environmental protection or improvement is FALSE, the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage is FALSE, the advancement of animal welfare is FALSE, reasonably regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o) is FALSE, the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god) is FALSE, for advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere is FALSE and purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes is FALSE.

